Hom(ZZdFactorization,ZZdFactorization,Symbol) -- the ZZ/d-graded homomorphism factorization between two ZZ/d-graded factorizations
**********************************************************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note Hom: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom,
  * Usage: 
        D = Hom(C1,C2,t)
  * Inputs:
      * C1, a *note ZZ/d graded factorization: ZZdFactorization,, or a *note
        module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,
      * C2, a *note ZZ/d graded factorization: ZZdFactorization,, or a *note
        module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,
      * t, a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, or a *note ring element:
        (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DegreeLimit: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom, => ..., default value null, 
      * *note MinimalGenerators: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom, => ..., default value true,
      * *note Strategy: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom, => ..., default value null, 
  * Outputs:
      * D, a *note ZZ/d graded factorization: ZZdFactorization,, the
        ZZ/d-graded factorization of homomorphisms between C1 and C2

Description
===========

The factorization of homomorphisms is a ZZ/d-graded factorization $D$ whose
$i$th component is the direct sum of $Hom(C1_j, C2_{k})$ over all $k-j = i \mod
d$. The differential on $Hom(C1_j, C2_{k})$ is the differential $Hom(id_{C1},
dd^{C2}) + t^j Hom(dd^{C1}, id_{C2})$ where $t$ is a primitive $d$th root of
unity.

The use of a primitive $d$th root of unity adds some subtlety to this
construction, since for $d > 2$ the user may need to adjoin a root of unity
using the \texttt{adjoinRoot} command. In general, if $C1$ is a factorizations
of $f$ and $C2$ is a factorization of $g$, then the Hom factorization
$\operatorname{Hom} (C1,C2)$ is a factorization of the difference $g-f$.

