Hom(ZZdFactorizationMap,ZZdFactorizationMap,Symbol) -- the map of factorizations between Hom complexes
******************************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note Hom: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom,
  * Usage: 
        h = Hom(f,g,t)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note map of ZZ/d-graded factorizations: ZZdFactorizationMap,, 
      * g, a *note map of ZZ/d-graded factorizations: ZZdFactorizationMap,, 
      * t, a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, optional input specifying the
        symbol used to represent the distinguished root of unity, required only
        if the underlying ring does not have a specified root of unity and the
        period is > 2
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DegreeLimit: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom, => ..., default value null, 
      * *note MinimalGenerators: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom, => ..., default value true,
      * *note Strategy: (Macaulay2Doc)Hom, => ..., default value null, 
  * Outputs:
      * h, a *note map of ZZ/d-graded factorizations: ZZdFactorizationMap,, 

Description
===========

The maps $f : C \to D$ and $g : E \to F$ of ZZ/d-graded factorizations induces
the map $h = Hom(f,g) : Hom(D,E) \to Hom(C,F)$ defined by $\phi \mapsto g \phi
f$. When the period of a factorization is greater than $2$, one should adjoin a
root of unity in order for the Hom factorization to be defined.

