|      x3yz2+xy2z3 0           |                                              |
|      0           x3yz2+xy2z3 |                                              |
|                                                                             |
|                                  /   Q[t]   \6                              |
|--------------------------------- |----------|  : 5                          |
|      0           0           |   | 2        |                               |
|      0           0           |   \t  - t + 1/                               |
|      0           0           |                                              |
|xy2z3 0           0           |                                              |
|      x3yz2+xy2z3 0           |                                              |
|      0           x3yz2+xy2z3 |                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

These linear factorizations are interesting because they give an explicit
construction of nontrivial Ulrich modules over hypersurface rings. These Ulrich
modules are constructed by taking the cokernel of any of the matrices in the
factorization, then tensoring with the hypersurface ring.

