randomTailMF -- Construct a matrix factorization from a high syzygy of a random module over a hypersurface
**********************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        randomTailMF(f, n, m, deg)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, A ring element
        representing a nonzerodivisor on the ambient ring
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, \( n \): The bound controlling
        the size of the presentation of the module
      * m, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, - \( m \): The bound
        controlling the size of the presentation of the module
      * deg, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, - \( d \): The degree bound
        on the generators
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * WellDefined (missing documentation) => ..., default value true, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ZZ/d graded factorization: ZZdFactorization,, A matrix
        factorization built from a high syzygy of a random coker presented as
        the cokernel of a map $R^m \to R^n$ with entries at most degree $d$.

Description
===========

If no degree is specified, then a random list of degrees with values ranging
from 1 to 10 is chosen. If no parameters on the presentation matrix are
specified, then random values between 1 and 10 are chosen. It is highly
recommended that one specifies bounds when working over a ring with more than 3
variables, since the computations may get out of hand if the presentation size
is randomly chosen to be too large. This function is mostly meant as a helper
function for generating interesting examples of matrix factorizations en masse.
Note that for certain parameters this function may take some time to run, since
the code will continue to generate examples until it obtains a well-defined
matrix factorization (if it does not do this, the ranks of the modules in
resulting factorization may not be equal).

