If a ring is used rather than a ring map, then the implicit map from the
underlying ring of the complex to the given ring is used.

+-------------------------------------+
|i10 : use R;                         |
+-------------------------------------+
|i11 : A = R/(x^2+y^2+z^2);           |
+-------------------------------------+
|i12 : C ** A                         |
|                                     |
|       4      4      4               |
|o12 = A  <-- A  <-- A                |
|                                     |
|      0      1      0                |
|                                     |
|o12 : ZZdFactorization               |
+-------------------------------------+
|i13 : assert(map(A,R) ** C == C ** A)|
+-------------------------------------+

The commutativity of tensor product is witnessed as follows.

+------------------------------+
|i14 : assert(D == C ** phi)   |
+------------------------------+
|i15 : assert(C ** A == A ** C)|
+------------------------------+

When the modules in the factorization are not free modules, this is different
than the image of a factorization under a ring map.

